\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For an integer n with 0<n<d and n not equal to d-1, if E is n-Ahlfors regular and all Riesz transforms of singularity n are bounded on $L^2(E)$, is E necessarily n-uniformly rectifiable?
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2011/36}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Let $0<n<d$ be integers with $n\neq d-1$, and let $E\subseteq\mathbb R^d$ be $n$-Ahlfors regular. If its $n$-dimensional Riesz transforms are bounded on $L^2(E)$, must $E$ be uniformly $n$-rectifiable? Ahlfors regularity means that its $n$-dimensional measure in balls is comparable to the $n$th power of their radius; uniform rectifiability requires quantitative large pieces of Lipschitz images.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 30001856 (OWR-11128-010). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Original problem formulation and mathematical context.
\url{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2011/36}.
\end{itemize}
\label{end:30001856}

